course: dictionary
lesson: 11721
title: Authorities: who is in charge?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one. «Please report the broken fence to the authorities.» Here authorities means the people in charge, such as the police or the council, who can fix or check things.
explain: «Dr Amara is one of the leading authorities on whales.» Here an authority is someone who knows a great deal about a subject. Ask yourself: is this about who is in charge, or who knows the most? The clue words tell you.
explain: Authorities is the plural of authority, which means more than one. Authority on its own can also mean the power or right to give orders or make decisions, as in «The referee has the authority to stop the game.» Try each meaning in the sentence and see which one fits.
fact: Authorities means the people or organisations in charge, such as the police, the government or the council.
fact: An authority is also an expert who knows a great deal about a subject, as in «an authority on birds».
fact: «The local authorities closed the road» means the council or people in charge closed it.
fact: In history or science, «authorities» can mean experts or trusted books.
exercise: «Please report the theft to the authorities.» What are the authorities here? | * The police and other people in charge | A group of friends who play football | The books in the library | Trees and plants
exercise: «Dr Amara is one of the leading authorities on whales.» What does authority mean here? | A person who is in charge of a road | A loud shout | * An expert who knows a great deal about the subject | A type of boat
exercise: Which word is the singular of authorities? | authorise | * authority | authentic | author
test: «The health authorities said everyone must wash their hands.» Who gave this advice? | The shopkeeper only | * The people in charge of health | Ghosts | Friends in the playground
test: «The school authorities decided the trip would go ahead.» What does this mean? | The children chose the trip alone | Nobody was in charge | * The people in charge at school made the decision | The trip was cancelled by the weather
exam: Ella says «authority» always means a loud shout. Is she right? | * No, it can mean the power to give orders, or an expert | No, it means a type of cloud | Yes, always | Yes, but only at school
